#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198392 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198034 :question/id #uuid "b81eb0cb-a740-5e86-921c-4baa8c179e55" :question/math-academy-id "e-12387" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y = h(x,z)$ for $(x,z) \\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $\\mathbf{F}(x,y,z) = \\left\\langle P,\\: Q,\\: R \\right\\rangle$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \n\\Bigg( \n\\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x} \n+ Q \n- \\bbox[3pt, border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}} \\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z}\n\\Bigg) \\: \\textrm{d}A,\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $\\mathbf j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $\\mathbf{r}(u,v)$ for $(u,v) \\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $\\mathbf{F}$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\textrm{d}\\mathbf{S}\n=\n\\iint\\limits_S \\mathbf{F} \\cdot \\mathbf{n} \\,\\textrm{d}S\n=\n\\iint\\limits_D \\mathbf{F}(\\mathbf{r}(u,v)) \\cdot (\\mathbf{r}'_u \\times \\mathbf{r}'_v) \\,\\textrm{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y = h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(x,z) = \\left\\langle x, \\: h(x,z),\\: z \\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $\\mathbf j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}'_z \\times \\mathbf{r}'_x = - \\mathbf{r}'_x \\times \\mathbf{r}'_z =   \\left \\langle -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial x}, \\: 1, \\: -\\dfrac{\\partial h}{\\partial z} \\right \\rangle\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\frac{∂h}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "55cd2fdb-4cae-5f0b-bea0-e845d51190e1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ae2d92e-ed1f-5f54-9f3e-4ce38b8da70d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P,\\,$ $∂P,\\,$ $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275570} {:answer/id #uuid "2786ad1c-2c77-5044-8bba-022ff896dea2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275839} {:answer/id #uuid "44ac2a5e-f444-5ab8-8d2f-b1befe6ae68f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276081} {:answer/id #uuid "4e5bb47b-fd98-5623-8225-b6a60c57026d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276170} {:answer/id #uuid "8b5ef7c7-aca0-5aa4-b9ce-95d7314ff4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276743}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8b5ef7c7-aca0-5aa4-b9ce-95d7314ff4ab" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P,\\,$ $∂g,\\,$ $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276743} :answer-field/id #uuid "62e5c477-cfc8-5f60-94c2-948eec13a409" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277901}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9923f940-6e67-51d8-8336-d46f1ce02fd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161166" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\frac{}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198605 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05adf01e-c7b1-5e97-b5eb-3f2025764ada" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275527} {:answer/id #uuid "54638f1d-1303-5a95-ba48-da06bfbcee4d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276226} {:answer/id #uuid "57c6c235-25f5-5f19-a313-895f424e6c10" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $P$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276263} {:answer/id #uuid "74d9839f-a860-523e-923f-a6741e1f1d1e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276535} {:answer/id #uuid "f604d50d-71c4-5c4d-a5df-37fe3a24fd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277629}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f604d50d-71c4-5c4d-a5df-37fe3a24fd07" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277629} :answer-field/id #uuid "1de77bc5-7a01-5bd2-8ed1-6d5e7458e367" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277779}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b8aa780b-8e8f-52b7-b96a-66254bcb10c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161189" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}+(-Q\\,)\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278512 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7082e8bb-b180-5b74-b8a7-e85890274ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-Q$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276491} {:answer/id #uuid "90563b64-bf19-5230-a35a-721a99920cf3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-Q$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276784} {:answer/id #uuid "91780ef2-9a71-57a1-99ea-245bc6f522d4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276795} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8f03ec-bd00-5c8c-b9b2-ca5f6b5f96f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277155} {:answer/id #uuid "ed73198a-0a2c-58e6-bfa9-608aaaadffc4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-R$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277561}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7082e8bb-b180-5b74-b8a7-e85890274ede" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-Q$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276491} :answer-field/id #uuid "2729d011-05e6-5f9a-aa2c-45fe935285a9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277789}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1aa60ad7-eedc-5a6c-9adc-f826e8cc15e1" :question/math-academy-id "q-161446" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(-Q\\,)\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\frac{∂k}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278515 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0643aa87-cd34-546b-808f-baf7069ddd3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275535} {:answer/id #uuid "33a51edf-b815-559f-bf1a-c079952737b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275936} {:answer/id #uuid "363514bb-3d3f-5b6d-8842-2ca7a25acfe7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275960} {:answer/id #uuid "608d35ab-75b6-598e-b2f0-82ca279404a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276340} {:answer/id #uuid "fb6360fb-9cce-5dca-8b8d-0788f42c0291" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2P$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277681}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0643aa87-cd34-546b-808f-baf7069ddd3f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$2P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275535} :answer-field/id #uuid "3292da62-41fc-5075-93e6-a9828fa8aabd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277805}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ce5730b3-fc65-5b4c-bdd5-95a34bf939c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161453" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=-{x}^{2}z$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\,xz+Q+\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=-{x}^{2}z,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,-{x}^{2}z,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,-2xz,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,-{x}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & -2xz & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{x}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨-2xz,\\,-1,\\,-{x}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨2xz,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(2P\\,xz+Q+R\\,{x}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1030e7c9-e96e-503f-967b-785d007daa76" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2Q$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275620} {:answer/id #uuid "144499aa-4e39-58cc-9e9a-8a50dd3383d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2Q$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275665} {:answer/id #uuid "68ab0b1a-abea-51fe-b67c-1548bc01d191" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276417} {:answer/id #uuid "a76e424f-dd86-5864-94a5-584b2a311108" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$Q$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276987} {:answer/id #uuid "c0b4e30b-9426-50d8-bc2f-4878ecf2feae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-Q$ and $-R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "144499aa-4e39-58cc-9e9a-8a50dd3383d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2Q$ and $-2R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275665} :answer-field/id #uuid "5e4956ee-4770-5be1-83ec-b19c8e91c7c7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277890}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a6603224-9e78-54fd-8272-af20dda792ce" :question/math-academy-id "q-161456" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=2yz$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P+\\,z+\\,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=2yz,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨2yz,\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨2z,\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨2y,\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 2z & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2y & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(y,z))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-2z,\\,-2y⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P+(\\,-2Q)\\,z+(\\,-2R)\\,y\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186278519 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11e71243-92a0-5d43-b5a3-d37e9d9577d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275638} {:answer/id #uuid "1733f81c-8c75-5995-ad54-2ab255bb13b5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂x},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275694} {:answer/id #uuid "2cfc931d-121f-57c0-ac5c-fdc2916d3343" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275883} {:answer/id #uuid "3a8033d3-73a6-5faf-9ba8-27ae7a3acd02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186275991} {:answer/id #uuid "4f2411ed-300b-54b9-9625-f35ce1fd17dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276182}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f2411ed-300b-54b9-9625-f35ce1fd17dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "Q,\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\text{ }\\frac{∂k}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186276182} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fceae9a-bc1a-5dd0-a3ba-10a17494da0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186277822}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "46dbd1e0-5399-5e33-b950-1f08b8d717ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-161465" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $x=k(y,z)$ for $(y,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(P-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}-R\\,\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $x=k(y,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(y,z)=⟨k(y,z),\\,y,\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,1,\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨\\frac{∂k}{∂z},\\,0,\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂y} & 1 & 0 \\\\ \\frac{∂k}{∂z} & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{y}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨1,\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂y},\\,-\\frac{∂k}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(P-Q\\,\\frac{∂k}{∂y}-R\\,\\frac{∂k}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186373979 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "333cd0c0-ea57-5e53-975d-79f76fb18013" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373981} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac4e1a7-5360-5598-85dd-70d2cbb3cd86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373982} {:answer/id #uuid "1e3427ae-61e2-5bd9-9b34-c9a3c4f9f4ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373983} {:answer/id #uuid "d4c19d76-6f69-56ba-b90f-4e4dfe1d7922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373984} {:answer/id #uuid "24bbe4da-5bb4-5f04-bd1a-ef46ed95ade9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂y},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d4c19d76-6f69-56ba-b90f-4e4dfe1d7922" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-P,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373984} :answer-field/id #uuid "2c9eea5b-e0c0-5c9a-bb50-8a16e942de63" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186373980}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "54d448ec-919c-5c61-a65c-48a634770024" :question/math-academy-id "q-161460" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=h(x,z)$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\,+Q-R\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=h(x,z),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,h(x,z),\\,z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\frac{∂h}{∂z},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & \\frac{∂h}{∂x} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{∂h}{∂z} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,-1,\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂h}{∂x},\\,1,\\,-\\frac{∂h}{∂z}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂x}+Q-R\\,\\frac{∂h}{∂z})\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401240 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b6ce404-ebcc-51cc-943b-4e4e79eb614d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2R$ and $-2P$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401242} {:answer/id #uuid "44cf614b-483e-57c6-b48c-bdea43bdf8c6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401243} {:answer/id #uuid "920e8870-c9e3-5c16-bd67-bc6987066749" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401244} {:answer/id #uuid "7051dd66-a8a1-5bd2-a47a-cbbfa2e9ad6a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $-R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401245} {:answer/id #uuid "23d98ea3-b274-5a12-a721-30dfede024ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401246}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "23d98ea3-b274-5a12-a721-30dfede024ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $6R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401246} :answer-field/id #uuid "d028cbae-7e56-54b5-84a6-c407d88b99d4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401241}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "75438633-2fed-5dc6-8f93-bd3571d872a5" :question/math-academy-id "q-161455" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $y=2x{z}^{3}$ for $(x,z)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{z}^{3}+Q-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,x{z}^{2})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?\n\n*Hint: The orientation of $S$ should be chosen such that the unit normal vector has a positive  $j$-component.*" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $y=2x{z}^{3},$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,z)=⟨x,\\,2x{z}^{3},z⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,2{z}^{3},\\,0⟩ \\\\ {r}_{z}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,6x{z}^{2},\\,1⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 2{z}^{3} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 6x{z}^{2} & 1\\end{aligned}|=⟨2{z}^{3},\\,-1,\\,6x{z}^{2}⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHowever, notice that the $j$-component is negative. Therefore, we select the normal vector that points in the *opposite* direction:\n\n$$\n{r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′}=-{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{z}^{′}=⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,z))\\cdot ({r}_{z}^{′}\\times {r}_{x}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,R\\,⟩\\cdot ⟨-2{z}^{3},\\,1,\\,-6x{z}^{2}⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2P\\,{z}^{3}+Q-6R\\,x{z}^{2}\\,)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401247 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0e4fc5fe-cb91-578b-8dbf-4a5ffd8c3fd5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂Q" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401249} {:answer/id #uuid "1b9077fe-1593-50c2-8f89-3d6b0dcabdfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401250} {:answer/id #uuid "1c8485ee-e4b0-5c7e-96b8-4bdcc10e86bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂z" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401251} {:answer/id #uuid "09583e22-9aba-582a-80e6-e8808ccbeebf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }-Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401252} {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3581b172-6284-5dbd-9767-909757324a62" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "∂g,\\text{ }Q,\\text{ }∂y" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401253} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ee6b27a-bcd5-52e8-9493-e839011ba707" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401248}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "417f7e3b-659c-5f7d-913c-decf384148f3" :question/math-academy-id "q-161457" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂x}-\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401254 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffab214-a531-5f16-9548-4aba64a14e09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂z$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401256} {:answer/id #uuid "27be7768-bf01-5a25-8f76-a7b3aa6827e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401257} {:answer/id #uuid "ef0b36ca-8d50-5d13-b2d4-01dff2d1189e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂z$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401258} {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401259} {:answer/id #uuid "401f8a2b-7596-5052-8ec8-449369280a45" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂y$ and $∂x$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401260}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8c295efa-f53d-5757-9ea8-624ce01afe74" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x$ and $∂y$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401259} :answer-field/id #uuid "0585e533-2d5e-5fa5-b53e-85ce1c70e498" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401255}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b2128236-dd4e-5cec-8d28-c07554df0f5e" :question/math-academy-id "q-161463" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\boxed{}}-Q\\frac{∂g}{\\boxed{}}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423121 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "65b0d9ca-824a-5dcc-9c38-6fd9b637f302" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4P$ and $-2Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423096} {:answer/id #uuid "92e3c6dc-2a2e-5a89-badd-5c261850de58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423102} {:answer/id #uuid "c4841ceb-b2a6-58c8-96a9-bc8ce5fca6d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-4Q$ and $P$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423106} {:answer/id #uuid "f0f8eac7-f04f-5d8d-a984-6504f0ab8abc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2R$ and $Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423112} {:answer/id #uuid "f233c1ca-3c84-5c07-9f36-b49ccdff39a8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$P$ and $-R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423113}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92e3c6dc-2a2e-5a89-badd-5c261850de58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$-2P$ and $-Q$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423102} :answer-field/id #uuid "b4de48c6-077d-5787-a098-9c9aedaea635" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423117}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "431ed508-ca53-50c3-b7a4-875fc9b23084" :question/math-academy-id "q-161449" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z={x}^{2}y$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,xy+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}\\,{x}^{2}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z={x}^{2}y,$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,{x}^{2}y⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,2xy⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,{x}^{2}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & 2xy \\\\ 0 & 1 & {x}^{2}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-2xy,\\,-{x}^{2},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-2xy,\\,-{x}^{2},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-2P\\,xy+(\\,-Q\\,)\\,{x}^{2}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423122 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45ff11c7-5335-5ffb-9240-826266b3b1ec" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂z,∂g,$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423089} {:answer/id #uuid "73c7f141-a638-5c2f-a226-4db1e183214c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x,∂g,$ and $-R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423097} {:answer/id #uuid "d23fc8da-8bb8-570e-94f8-2dd437e7348c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x,∂g,$ and $1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423109} {:answer/id #uuid "f0b4e080-429e-59c9-b7b0-c3e775d0e61a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x,∂g,$ and $-1$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423111} {:answer/id #uuid "f2a31230-1787-5f1a-a9cf-ba9794a07b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x,∂g,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423114}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f2a31230-1787-5f1a-a9cf-ba9794a07b89" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$∂x,∂g,$ and $R$" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423114} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a3936a3-b7fb-5980-bcc0-5a21e600fc1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423115}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b08ff1d-1edf-523c-a8e8-287f135b6040" :question/math-academy-id "q-162607" :question/problem "For a surface $S$ given by $z=g(x,y)$ for $(x,y)\\in D,$ the flux of the vector field $F(x,y,z)=⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}-Q\\frac{\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}}}{∂y}+\\bbox[3pt,border:1pt solid black]{\\phantom{A}})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nFrom left to right, what are the missing parts of the formula?" :question/worked-solution "For a surface $S$ with parametrization $r(u,v)$ for $(u,v)\\in D,$ the flux of a vector field $F$ over $S$ is given by\n\n$$\n{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S={∬}_{S}F\\cdot n\\,\\text{d}S={∬}_{D}F(r(u,v))\\cdot ({r}_{u}^{′}\\times {r}_{v}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nSince our surface $S$ is of the form $z=g(x,y),$ it can be parameterized as\n\n$$\nr(x,y)=⟨x,\\,y,\\,g(x,y)⟩.\n$$\n\nWe now compute the tangent vectors to the grid curves:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′} & =⟨1,\\,0,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂x}⟩ \\\\ {r}_{y}^{′} & =⟨0,\\,1,\\,\\frac{∂g}{∂y}⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, the fundamental vector product is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′} & =|\\begin{aligned}i & j & k \\\\ 1 & 0 & \\frac{∂g}{∂x} \\\\ 0 & 1 & \\frac{∂g}{∂y}\\end{aligned}|=⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the surface integral can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{S}F\\cdot \\text{d}S & ={∬}_{D}F(r(x,y))\\cdot ({r}_{x}^{′}\\times {r}_{y}^{′})\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}⟨P,\\,Q,\\,R⟩\\cdot ⟨-\\frac{∂g}{∂x},\\,-\\frac{∂g}{∂y},\\,1⟩\\,\\text{d}A \\\\  & ={∬}_{D}(-P\\frac{∂g}{∂x}-Q\\frac{∂g}{∂y}+R)\\,\\text{d}A.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Expressing Flux Over a Surface as a Repeated Integral Using a Formula"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198397 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198039 :question/id #uuid "d123b7fd-5a13-50c0-aa8c-dc196f4b1bcd" :question/math-academy-id "e-12406" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in Cartesian $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:1 \\leq xy \\leq  2, \\: 1 \\leq y \\leq 2, \\: 0 \\leq z\\leq 1 \\big\\}.\n$$\n\nBy performing the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,  \\qquad\nv=y, \\qquad\nw=2z,\n$$\n\n \n\nwrite down the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint\\limits_R 3xy^2+6yz \\, \\mathrm{d}V\n$$\n\nas a repeated integral in terms of $u,$ $v,$ and $w.$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/c3/c3c03fec53fda9106ca287f15afd0d07b1adddf00675b2bdbec8a658fbdf06aa.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=xy,  \\qquad\nv=y, \\qquad\nw=2z,\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/64/643fb7a9bbc46ac2a6fe8c6d89f4fcb7f2db206c7af11f7cde9e1a34da8ac201.png)\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,1\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}y & x & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2|\\begin{aligned}y & x \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =2(y-0) \\\\  & =2y \\\\  & =2v.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} =\\dfrac{1}{2v}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}3x{y}^{2}+6yz\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(3uv+3vw)\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}3v(u+w)\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2v}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\frac{3}{2}(u+w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a898f345-9210-5976-9a42-f415a0e7eaf5" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198959 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "aa28a111-7c64-5cae-a44e-26804e62c591" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{u v}3 wdwdvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197625} {:answer/id #uuid "b8d0634a-8719-5b2d-899f-01630da9b099" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}3 w 2dwdvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197626} {:answer/id #uuid "cb9c283f-7f20-5d2c-b016-e8c65dd68fae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{u v}3 w 2dwdvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197627} {:answer/id #uuid "3597f84c-12de-55c9-b1c4-2e0cc9496766" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}3 wdwdvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197628} {:answer/id #uuid "6a4f2c75-bf76-581c-bac3-60a655293d9e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{2}\\displaystyle \\int_{1}^{u + v}3 wdwdvdu" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197629}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fbe99af2-8c2c-533c-80a0-ca42ec737c5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197624}] :question/id #uuid "96fa59d6-c144-5393-a4a7-7b8198eb987c" :question/math-academy-id "q-38941" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 1 \\le x + y \\le 2, 1 \\le x - y \\le 2, 1 \\le z \\le (x - y)(x + y)}$.\nBy performing the change of variables\n$u = x + y, v = x - y, w = \\sqrt{z}$,\nthe triple integral\n$∭_(R)3\\sqrt{z}dV$\ncan be written in terms of $u$, $v$, and $w$ as"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "efccd2f5-3dcb-549b-a791-9a953c0859ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197649} {:answer/id #uuid "01110ca9-4f16-5a77-ade5-867fbcd28671" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197650} {:answer/id #uuid "28f56d8e-e1c7-5447-a918-701e450eabeb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197651} {:answer/id #uuid "46f32d0b-96ea-5d4d-8468-c29158bdc3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197652} {:answer/id #uuid "721547b0-a678-5c59-b301-7880bb72ccba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\sin u\\cos v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197653}] :answer-field/id #uuid "92bb2c37-88bf-542c-b72c-3da1ea32bcd2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197648}] :question/id #uuid "9d27a7ac-3737-5071-b997-af83c998bd7d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39054" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): \\frac{3π}{4} \\le 2x + y \\le π, 0 \\le y + z \\le \\frac{π}{2},-1 \\le z \\le 1}$.\nGiven the change of variables\n$u = 2x + y, v = y + z, w = z$,\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n$∭_(R)\\sin (2x + y)\\cos (y + z)dV = \\displaystyle \\int_{3π/4}^{π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{-1}^{1}A A A A A Adwdvdu$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374083 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "19590723-9dbc-594f-9f4e-6ca0160553fe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{u}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374085} {:answer/id #uuid "e319ca87-b89a-5147-977c-a329bf0ff49e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u{e}^{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374086} {:answer/id #uuid "e89988d6-d0a3-55a0-b505-58a6138e15f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{v{e}^{u}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374087} {:answer/id #uuid "26fa2b15-0257-541e-aadc-bc6d9526fdc6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{v}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374088} {:answer/id #uuid "fbfe48d4-fab5-5337-a025-f32c4db8dd7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u{e}^{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e319ca87-b89a-5147-977c-a329bf0ff49e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u{e}^{v}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374086} :answer-field/id #uuid "9448f758-7aaf-5272-b5f6-88f1b32ee8d2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374084}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2efc4cf1-75d6-520b-94c1-d854f8fd8b64" :question/math-academy-id "q-161880" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le x+y\\le 2,\\,0\\le y-x\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}2(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+y,\\,v=y-x,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n   \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(1+1) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}2(x+y){e}^{y-x}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}2u{e}^{v}\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}2u{e}^{v}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\,u{e}^{v}\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186375123 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e650b073-7e42-5a29-a5f0-49b40ec48ce8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375125} {:answer/id #uuid "5769b11c-c825-5eeb-8948-b8c15db36333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375126} {:answer/id #uuid "e83ba63d-1a5b-5b95-80a3-c20f0bf16584" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375127} {:answer/id #uuid "2be95ca7-c78a-5b21-980c-e49c00d94791" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2u\\operatorname{ln}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375128} {:answer/id #uuid "976dc3c1-b29e-52db-a43c-419b89323c0e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375129}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5769b11c-c825-5eeb-8948-b8c15db36333" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375126} :answer-field/id #uuid "e86f996d-8724-5990-98a1-29212aad3144" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186375124}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c4bdde5-7a56-510d-ac15-d76aed6084be" :question/math-academy-id "q-39057" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by \n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,1\\le {x}^{2}+y\\le 2,\\,-1\\le x\\le 1,\\,1\\le z\\le 4}.\n$$\n\n \n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=2x+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(2x+z)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$ \n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+y,\\,v=2x+z,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,1\\le u\\le 2,\\,-1\\le v\\le 6,\\,1\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$ \n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-(2-0) \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(2x+z)\\operatorname{ln}⁡({x}^{2}+y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{2}{\\int }_{-1}^{6}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\,\\frac{1}{2}v\\operatorname{ln}⁡u\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186401302 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e70215a-59ba-5fc4-8869-08fd079debf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401304} {:answer/id #uuid "2e6baa46-2362-5360-8c5f-7031d62e1411" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡v\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401305} {:answer/id #uuid "7adad8b8-d3ef-52ef-b487-e4018ac1507b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{cos}^{2}⁡(u-v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401306} {:answer/id #uuid "06bed278-63d1-5662-b5c3-13bc65e1ffc5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401307} {:answer/id #uuid "03678aa6-e1ae-5bec-8d2a-faf8e1de8ec2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}{cos}^{2}⁡(uv)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v-u" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401308}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e70215a-59ba-5fc4-8869-08fd079debf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401304} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b627816-1293-53a9-a8e8-93bf2fb0fcef" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186401303}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4004275e-59e4-54d2-bbaa-29203ac29308" :question/math-academy-id "q-161879" :question/problem "Consider the finite region $R$ in the $xyz$-space, given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z-3y\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le z\\le 2}.\n$$\n\nGiven the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the missing part of the expression below?\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo find the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+3y,\\,v=z-3y,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo find the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,0\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 3 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1|\\begin{aligned}-3 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-3-0) \\\\  & =-3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{cos}^{2}⁡(x+z)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(z-3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}\\,\\frac{1}{3}{cos}^{2}⁡(u+v)\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\,dw\\,dv\\,du\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Change of Variables in a Triple Integral"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198398 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198040 :question/id #uuid "3fccb151-d67f-525b-8804-62589913084a" :question/math-academy-id "e-12407" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \n\\iiint \\limits_R (x+y^2)^{15}z \\: \\mathrm{d}V,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\:  0 \\leq x+y^2 \\leq 1, \\: 0 \\leq y+z \\leq 4, \\: 0\\leq z\\leq 2 \\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/eb/eb8f40c6ffabf36ae5a305c7c7b71683b914b537f64766a20aac09d3666fac81.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y^2, \\qquad\nv=  y+z, \\qquad\nw = 2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/4b/4bcd5430181c2cb411dfe984b04399f2f80ab9d1747d5cf82596d105419b2b4f.png)\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 4,\\,0\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(x+{y}^{2})^{15}z\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}\\cdot \\frac{w}{2}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{∭}_{Δ}{u}^{15}w\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{\\int }_{0}^{4}{u}^{15}w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{u}^{15}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}w\\,dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot [\\frac{{u}^{16}}{16}]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=4}\\cdot [\\frac{{w}^{2}}{2}]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\cdot (\\frac{1}{16}-0)\\cdot (4-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "832dc2b8-873f-56c6-bd87-5e886a586fb1" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198623 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8f646c61-db32-5905-849a-04ada136657a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194370} {:answer/id #uuid "dda01489-76a5-59ec-9704-87adeb52fd5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194371} {:answer/id #uuid "18c290d7-6b8d-50ae-99c2-f0eb7045b012" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194372} {:answer/id #uuid "1935b921-c755-5f9b-b2fd-fdf3102bd3a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194373} {:answer/id #uuid "494337ce-d303-53d0-97cd-b0e1b611d95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194374}] :answer-field/id #uuid "cb5cc458-53cc-5d59-888b-d4167a5b9c4d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194369}] :question/id #uuid "1d5d9726-730c-531d-b668-66f3dda1040e" :question/math-academy-id "q-162139" :question/problem "Use the change of variables\n$u = 3x - 2y, v = y + 2z, w = z$\nto evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)(3x - 2y)^{2}(y + 2z)dV$,\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 0 \\le 3x - 2y \\le 3, 0 \\le y + 2z \\le 2, 0 \\le 2z \\le 1}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ef7332e4-b981-54af-8da2-fcdb494e9902" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197655} {:answer/id #uuid "281c6df6-036f-5860-be8c-11e9d432022a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197656} {:answer/id #uuid "467be8d9-d070-5455-96ad-bb6a53f6902c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln 2}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197657} {:answer/id #uuid "7557b355-b5e6-5c35-9ec5-ce7baaeba82d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197658} {:answer/id #uuid "7a8c87e9-0a3c-552b-81b8-0ac086467daf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ln 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197659}] :answer-field/id #uuid "9eb66677-2e9a-5652-8302-63d8739cb7ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197654}] :question/id #uuid "3982f9b1-ca56-5e4b-8f97-4f23fb6f2fc3" :question/math-academy-id "q-39055" :question/problem "Use the change of variables\n$u = x^{2} + y, v = x + z, w = z$\nto evaluate the triple integral\n$∭_(R)(x + z)\\cos (x^{2} + y)dV$,\nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): \\frac{π}{6} \\le x^{2} + y \\le \\frac{π}{2}, 0 \\le x + z \\le 2,-1 \\le z \\le 1}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374090 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83465347-83b5-56e1-942c-234dfe757ae5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374092} {:answer/id #uuid "612ab49b-15d8-51e5-aae7-a61849ec93f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374093} {:answer/id #uuid "a65ba9a5-15e7-5ad8-a491-1c88d583b06c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374094} {:answer/id #uuid "329bb962-4b70-560b-b47c-55ff92416c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374095} {:answer/id #uuid "4bb73704-113c-5943-812c-4fa342511784" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374096}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "329bb962-4b70-560b-b47c-55ff92416c5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374095} :answer-field/id #uuid "b2769a2e-b298-59d8-a66c-56d53b56460b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374091}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d79ae16-602a-5f97-985b-2aa915324df2" :question/math-academy-id "q-161916" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+2y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-z)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le {x}^{2}+2y\\le 2,\\,0\\le x-z\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+2y,\\,v=x-z,\\,w=z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le \\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,-1\\le w\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =0-2 \\\\  & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}({x}^{2}+2y)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(x-z)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}u\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{2}u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[\\frac{{u}^{2}}{2}]_{u=0}^{u=2}\\cdot {[\\operatorname{sin}⁡v]}_{v=0}^{v=\\pi /2}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(2-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1) \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374097 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6bab9309-d8a5-51d1-807c-f4b9da8a32be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374099} {:answer/id #uuid "5ce54197-6e08-581c-ba9a-fae72f50566a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374100} {:answer/id #uuid "25b0799f-0058-5d49-9ffd-855eeeab2145" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374101} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f508fc-00c9-564f-b3b2-26dd372c3765" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374102} {:answer/id #uuid "ab6c269d-ee4e-5268-bed1-d76fc26084c1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374103}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6bab9309-d8a5-51d1-807c-f4b9da8a32be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374099} :answer-field/id #uuid "77c9eaf7-9a6d-5842-bcbc-caffd6461b49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374098}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "18953d2e-5477-5ba3-a50e-218374e50b08" :question/math-academy-id "q-161917" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3}\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le \\frac{2x-y}{2}\\le 1,\\,0\\le \\frac{y}{2}\\le 2,\\,0\\le z\\le 3}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=\\frac{2x-y}{2},\\,v=\\frac{y}{2},\\,w=\\frac{z}{3}\n$$\n\n \n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 2,\\,0\\le w\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & -\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & \\,\\,\\,\\frac{1}{2} & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=6.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(\\frac{2x-y}{2}+\\frac{z}{3})\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}(u+w)\\cdot |6|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,(6u+6w)\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}[6uw+3{w}^{2}]_{w=0}^{w=1}\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6u(1-0)+3({1}^{2}-{0}^{2}))\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{2}(6u+3)\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6u+3)[v]_{v=0}^{v=2}\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(6u+3)\\cdot 2\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}(12u+6)\\,du \\\\  & =[6{u}^{2}+6u]_{u=0}^{u=1} \\\\  & =6({1}^{2}-{0}^{2})+6(1-0) \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374125 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae83ac8f-234c-5acd-bbb5-b25a1cc99939" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{2}({e}^{3}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374127} {:answer/id #uuid "8b34f7d1-a718-5683-99cc-2aea4d235600" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374128} {:answer/id #uuid "d20aea26-e03e-599a-8c57-858eaa89a312" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({e}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374129} {:answer/id #uuid "eecb9e17-6350-5926-b72c-9db686768a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4({e}^{3}-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374130} {:answer/id #uuid "c52a153b-0694-5e0b-973b-21e39ee2b46c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374131}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c52a153b-0694-5e0b-973b-21e39ee2b46c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2({e}^{3}-1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374131} :answer-field/id #uuid "6c5a98d3-da89-51a1-baff-f599217e67d7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0352be10-03ed-5d12-9e9a-501335a3d9bb" :question/math-academy-id "q-161929" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=2z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}(y+z){e}^{x+{y}^{2}}\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x+{y}^{2}\\le 3,\\,0\\le y+z\\le 1,\\,\\,0\\le z\\le 4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+{y}^{2},\\,v=y+z,\\,w=2z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 3,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 2y & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1\\cdot 1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}(y+z){e}^{x+{y}^{2}}\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}v{e}^{u}\\cdot |\\frac{1}{2}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{2}v{e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{3}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{8}\\frac{1}{2}v{e}^{u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{3}{e}^{u}\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{8}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[{e}^{u}]_{u=0}^{u=3}\\cdot {[\\frac{{v}^{2}}{2}]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{3}-1)\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}-0)\\cdot (8-0) \\\\  & =2({e}^{3}-1)\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374132 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae1b875c-d159-5d2e-8f13-b0a11a49b1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374134} {:answer/id #uuid "d522bb1b-a6e0-542c-b287-0eb7cc75c928" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374135} {:answer/id #uuid "d66c99b3-72d2-5b51-a1f5-0cf6426bc1f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374136} {:answer/id #uuid "c566677a-3ae4-5a9b-809b-312542dd7afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374137} {:answer/id #uuid "c62d12ae-e78b-5b54-b6e4-3414b80f62a4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374138}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c566677a-3ae4-5a9b-809b-312542dd7afb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374137} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc458f63-8f78-5a90-bd9d-b73e6cf7b6dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374133}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f98ed60b-0dd5-5ecd-92ff-418213f8aa92" :question/math-academy-id "q-161931" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu=2x+3y,\\,v=-2x+z,\\,w=\\frac{z}{2}\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+3y)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le 2x+3y\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le -2x+z\\le 1,\\,0\\le z\\le 6}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=2x+3y,\\,v=-2x+z,\\,w=\\frac{z}{2}\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\le u\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,0\\le w\\le 3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}\\,2 & 3 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 3 \\\\ -2 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(0-(-6)) \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}{z}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x+3y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}4{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\cdot |\\frac{1}{3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{4}{3}{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{4}{3}{w}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /6}^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{3}\\frac{4{w}^{2}}{3}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{sin}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /6}^{u=2\\pi }\\cdot {[v]}_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [\\frac{4{w}^{3}}{9}]_{w=0}^{w=3} \\\\  & =(0-\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (12-0) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374139 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "198c77be-33a4-5cbd-aa11-6bdd4191dd77" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374141} {:answer/id #uuid "6556381a-7067-5b49-b676-6d3c5d67e172" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374142} {:answer/id #uuid "9e51b078-3e9f-5dea-a9af-d596ee26bc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374143} {:answer/id #uuid "ddfb13d8-becf-5996-837a-11efb07fcec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374144} {:answer/id #uuid "da79f3cb-6b50-5ceb-bf5c-54aa4f670f09" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374145}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ddfb13d8-becf-5996-837a-11efb07fcec8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374144} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbb48d5b-5877-5dca-aa67-f0774b0d5c49" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "954ec2ce-743f-514f-9ac4-50e6ba28f6e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-161934" :question/problem "Use the change of variables \n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+2y,\\,v=x-2z,\\,w=4z\n$$\n\nto evaluate the triple integral\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dV,\n$$\n\n \nwhere the region $R$ is given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le {x}^{2}+2y\\le \\pi ,\\,\\pi \\le x-2z\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le z\\le 1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Let's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required integral, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nNote that the change of variables\n\n$$\nu={x}^{2}+2y,\\,v=x-2z,\\,w=4z\n$$\n\ngives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required integral, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le u\\le \\pi ,\\,\\pi \\le v\\le 2\\pi ,\\,0\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}2x & 2 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 0 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}2x & 2 \\\\ 1 & 0\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =4(0-2) \\\\  & =-8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{8}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(x-2z)\\operatorname{sin}⁡({x}^{2}+2y)\\,dV & ={∭}_{Δ}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{8}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={∭}_{Δ}\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{4}\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}{\\int }_{\\pi /2}^{\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡u\\,du\\cdot {\\int }_{\\pi }^{2\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡v\\,dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{4}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡u]_{u=\\pi /2}^{u=\\pi }\\cdot {[-\\operatorname{cos}⁡v]}_{v=\\pi }^{v=2\\pi }\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}(1+0)\\cdot (-1+(-1))\\cdot (4-0) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Evaluating a Triple Integral Using a Change of Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198399 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198041 :question/id #uuid "e00a8e15-e738-5fee-80f7-405844fa1422" :question/math-academy-id "e-12408" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR = \\big\\{ (x,y,z) \\: : \\: 0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\:-2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\: 1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8 \\big\\}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV = \\iiint\\limits_R \\textrm{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $\\mathbf{T}$ as follows:\n\n![](images/54/54035a0d0a2dd88eb0b4b89bb1162df2cf9b450a4ea4d5b5020163053880016f.png)\n\nThis transformation maps some region $\\Delta$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\iiint\\limits_{R} f(x,y,z) \\ \\mathrm{d}V = \\iiint\\limits_{\\Delta} \\ f(x(u,v,w), y(u,v,w), z(u,v,w)) \\: \\left| \\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\right|  \\: \\textrm{d}u \\textrm{d}v \\textrm{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf{T}.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0 \\leq x+y \\leq 1, \\qquad\n-2 \\leq y-2x \\leq 2, \\qquad\n1 \\leq z^3 \\leq 8,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu = x+y, \\qquad\nv = y-2x, \\qquad\nw = z^3.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function $\\mathbf T^{-1},$ that is\n\n![](images/4b/4bcd5430181c2cb411dfe984b04399f2f80ab9d1747d5cf82596d105419b2b4f.png)\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $\\Delta,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of $\\mathbf{T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-2\\le v\\le 2,\\,1\\le w\\le 8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 3{z}^{2}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =3{z}^{2}(-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =9{z}^{2} \\\\  & =9{w}^{2/3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T{:}$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $\\mathbf T$ is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} = \\left( \\dfrac{\\partial (u, v, w)}{\\partial (x, y, z)} \\right)^{-1} = \\dfrac 19 w^{-2/3}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\dfrac{\\partial (x, y, z)}{\\partial (u, v, w)} \\neq 0$ everywhere inside $\\Delta.$ In other words, $\\mathbf{T}$ has no critical points inside $\\Delta.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{9}{w}^{-2/3}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{1}^{8}\\frac{1}{9}{w}^{-2/3}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}{\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-2}^{2}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{8}{w}^{-2/3}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}[u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [v]_{-2}^{2}\\cdot [3{w}^{1/3}]_{1}^{8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (2-(-2))\\cdot 3({8}^{1/3}-{1}^{1/3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a8a79d4e-f9e0-583e-8e52-aa60c14fd4a3" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198624 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8865e6bb-6344-5115-83bd-d794ad8a6d9f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194376} {:answer/id #uuid "af38e17d-9eab-5171-abb0-c3baabb6b6ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194377} {:answer/id #uuid "11c289d0-561a-554e-a828-5cbceec4f39a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194378} {:answer/id #uuid "71cd64af-d215-54eb-bc30-68cf19a7e94a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194379} {:answer/id #uuid "792612f0-0e7e-56ef-ad2a-ff27287e9ee7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194380}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b34c2735-f65f-5fac-8644-8b3f3758b0a1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194375} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a834bf3-3872-5b5d-969c-61edac983b6b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374147} {:answer/id #uuid "775b3096-ba1d-5165-b372-dbf63725687c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374148} {:answer/id #uuid "03c2babe-4839-5bdc-afc4-6749f697b54b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374149} {:answer/id #uuid "42f92fc3-0b2e-5275-897a-62f665a73321" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374150} {:answer/id #uuid "fe97e990-5c5d-5501-b58a-2aaf7be8b50a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374151}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "775b3096-ba1d-5165-b372-dbf63725687c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374148} :answer-field/id #uuid "6efacc92-066c-57b7-8f28-3ccd8c8edf81" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374146}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "51e25390-3ec9-57e6-8340-3c34fe595d12" :question/math-academy-id "q-162149" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le 2y+z\\le 1,\\,1\\le y-2z\\le 4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,-1\\le 2y+z\\le 1,\\,1\\le y-2z\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=2y+z,\\,w=y-2z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,-1\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-\\frac{1}{5}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-\\frac{1}{5}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{1}{5}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}{\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}dw \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}[u]_{0}^{1}\\cdot [v]_{-1}^{1}\\cdot [w]_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1))\\cdot (4-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198965 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "881b0dd0-01c6-5013-9685-0e58c6da71c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197661} {:answer/id #uuid "c8a11a8a-6503-5229-93d1-630340faec00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197662} {:answer/id #uuid "33e5fb8a-a7fa-5959-b381-9183f9303802" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197663} {:answer/id #uuid "3404d280-8cad-5644-8ec1-3e6e08824288" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197664} {:answer/id #uuid "4cd7243b-48c8-50fa-aaa2-6aa1ef439e80" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197665}] :answer-field/id #uuid "3cd3b3e1-98ae-532b-94b3-c5b9b8ab641b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186197660}] :question/id #uuid "8b45928b-ff82-5faf-a3d8-f16998390abd" :question/math-academy-id "q-39060" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R$, given by\n$R = {(x, y, z): 1 \\le y \\le 2, 1 \\le \\sqrt{y} + x \\le 2, 1 \\le \\frac{1}{z + 1} \\le 5}$."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374152 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b2d1ca3-fd9b-57f2-ac29-4b16c135420c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "28" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374154} {:answer/id #uuid "d3fd9c37-4c27-5746-b851-f7b4512dd4ea" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374155} {:answer/id #uuid "a9c9008f-7ebb-5f0c-9ef4-3aba8c68542d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374156} {:answer/id #uuid "f9d1a911-155e-5064-989c-bdd887a93726" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "14" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374157} {:answer/id #uuid "730b3a0c-ab79-5dd0-b3b4-eb287d28f6b6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374158}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a9c9008f-7ebb-5f0c-9ef4-3aba8c68542d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374156} :answer-field/id #uuid "5287f396-f7c5-5f63-8e16-6e1738da83ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374153}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b20c6475-5823-5b74-865d-949b70ed5189" :question/math-academy-id "q-162155" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\,1\\le xz\\le 2}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}\\le x\\le 1,\\,3\\le y+{x}^{2}\\le 10,\\,1\\le xz\\le 2,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=y+{x}^{2},\\,w=xz.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,\\frac{1}{2}\\le u\\le 1,\\,3\\le v\\le 10,\\,1\\le w\\le 2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 1 & 0 \\\\ z & 0 & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & x\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =x \\\\  & =u.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=\\frac{1}{u}.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |\\frac{1}{u}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}{\\int }_{3}^{10}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\frac{1}{u}\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1/2}^{1}\\frac{1}{u}du\\cdot {\\int }_{3}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{2}dw \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡u]_{u=1/2}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=3}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=1}^{w=2} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{1}{2})\\cdot (10-3)\\cdot (2-1) \\\\  & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374159 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74cbe566-120c-5aa2-9d0f-8b0d51b92825" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374161} {:answer/id #uuid "372e9f40-cc59-532c-a782-6948b90925c9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374162} {:answer/id #uuid "8a16bd5e-23ba-5f37-8518-594f3a0cc660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374163} {:answer/id #uuid "455911a2-33b2-56a3-a5e6-863976f27387" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374164} {:answer/id #uuid "fe23f6e2-faba-5fcc-a1dc-a65a5472035a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374165}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a16bd5e-23ba-5f37-8518-594f3a0cc660" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374163} :answer-field/id #uuid "081961bd-b52b-565f-b46b-523fd43cde20" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374160}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2f54bd34-6966-5956-b7d3-23078157be35" :question/math-academy-id "q-162174" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n0\\le x\\le 1,\\,0\\le x+y\\le 1,\\,-1\\le z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v=x+y,\\,w=z+{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,0\\le u\\le 1,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,-1\\le w\\le 1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 2x & 2y & 1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{-1}^{1}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=0}^{u=1}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [w]_{w=-1}^{w=1} \\\\  & =(1-0)\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (1-(-1)) \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374166 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1020cd0b-a734-523c-a873-ae4ccc88f60a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374168} {:answer/id #uuid "eded55eb-08dc-5eea-9038-37f16794d4b0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "120" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374169} {:answer/id #uuid "5a6e2d78-660c-5283-ab55-0c4291532b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374170} {:answer/id #uuid "9479d209-1358-5b6d-b729-5097d39ca7d5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374171} {:answer/id #uuid "db3a4d38-29e5-54a0-b850-59abf8d19cfe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "200" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374172}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a6e2d78-660c-5283-ab55-0c4291532b27" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374170} :answer-field/id #uuid "11699b7d-5958-5ded-80ec-84552796d392" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374167}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b0695fc2-3499-5ac5-90f7-7e4077884682" :question/math-academy-id "q-162212" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-5\\le x+z\\le 5,\\,0\\le y\\le 10,\\,0\\le y-z\\le 6}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-5\\le x+z\\le 5,,\\,0\\le y\\le 10,\\,0\\le y-z\\le 6,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x+z,\\,v=y,\\,w=y-z.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-5\\le u\\le 5,\\,0\\le v\\le 10,\\,0\\le w\\le 6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -1\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-1.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-1|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}{\\int }_{0}^{10}{\\int }_{0}^{6}dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-5}^{5}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{10}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{6}dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-5}^{u=5}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=10}\\cdot [w]_{w=0}^{w=6} \\\\  & =(5-(-5))\\cdot (10-0)\\cdot (6-0) \\\\  & =600.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186374173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cedf6411-1c50-521c-9753-b78164606edd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374175} {:answer/id #uuid "2156f8ec-e04e-51b8-bc96-f4f57ab9bd72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374176} {:answer/id #uuid "641c62ce-a323-535d-adec-144d3026c01d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "36" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374177} {:answer/id #uuid "08e58611-c6b0-57ca-af8a-daaeefda2147" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374178} {:answer/id #uuid "652a7c70-07d1-5457-a185-ceed917c21bf" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2156f8ec-e04e-51b8-bc96-f4f57ab9bd72" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374176} :answer-field/id #uuid "230795bb-1c46-5274-8505-2f18821e3031" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186374174}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "df52d1f2-fb75-5e2b-acc7-74be8b4ade91" :question/math-academy-id "q-162213" :question/problem "Find the volume bounded by the region $R,$ given by\n\n$$\nR={(x,y,z)\\,:\\,-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that the volume of $R$ can be found as \n\n$$\nV={∭}_{R}\\text{d}V.\n$$\n\nLet's define a transformation $T$ as follows:\n\n$$\nT:[\\begin{aligned}u \\\\ v \\\\ w\\end{aligned}]⟶[\\begin{aligned}x(u,v,w) \\\\ y(u,v,w) \\\\ z(u,v,w)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nThis transformation maps some region $Δ$ in the $uvw$-space to our region $R$ in the $xyz$-space.\n\nTo compute the required volume, we can use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n{∭}_{R}f(x,y,z)\\text{ }dV={∭}_{Δ}\\text{ }f(x(u,v,w),y(u,v,w),z(u,v,w))\\,|\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w\n$$\n\nwhere $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}$ is the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T.$\n\nThe inequalities that define the region $R,$\n\n$$\n-2\\le x\\le 2,\\,0\\le {x}^{2}-y-z\\le 1,\\,1\\le \\sqrt{z}\\le 4,\n$$\n\nsuggest that we use the change of variables\n\n$$\nu=x,\\,v={x}^{2}-y-z,\\,w=\\sqrt{z}.\n$$\n\nThis change of variables gives us the *inverse* function ${T}^{-1},$ that is\n\n$$\n{T}^{-1}:[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]\\to [\\begin{aligned}u(x,y,z) \\\\ v(x,y,z) \\\\ w(x,y,z)\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nTo compute the required volume, we proceed in four steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find $Δ,$ which is the image of $R$ under the action of ${T}^{-1}.$ \n\nOur domain in the $uvw$-space is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Δ & ={(u,v,w)\\,:\\,-2\\le u\\le 2,\\,0\\le v\\le 1,\\,1\\le w\\le 4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 2**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to ${T}^{-1}$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)} & =|\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂u}{∂x} & \\frac{∂u}{∂y} & \\frac{∂u}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂v}{∂x} & \\frac{∂v}{∂y} & \\frac{∂v}{∂z} \\\\ \\frac{∂w}{∂x} & \\frac{∂w}{∂y} & \\frac{∂w}{∂z}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =|\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 2x & -1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =1(-1)^{1+1}|\\begin{aligned}-1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & \\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}}\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{z}} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2w}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n**Step 3**: Compute the Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T:$\n\nThe Jacobian determinant corresponding to $T$ is\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}={(\\frac{∂(u,v,w)}{∂(x,y,z)})}^{-1}=-2w.\n$$\n\nNote that $\\frac{∂(x,y,z)}{∂(u,v,w)}\\ne 0$ everywhere inside $Δ.$ In other words, $T$ has no critical points inside $Δ.$\n\n**Step 4**: Perform the change of variables:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∭}_{R}dV & ={∭}_{Δ}1\\cdot |-2w|\\,\\text{d}u\\text{d}v\\text{d}w \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}{\\int }_{0}^{1}{\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw\\,dv\\,du \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-2}^{2}du\\cdot {\\int }_{0}^{1}dv\\cdot {\\int }_{1}^{4}2w\\,dw \\\\  & =[u]_{u=-2}^{u=2}\\cdot [v]_{v=0}^{v=1}\\cdot [{w}^{2}]_{w=1}^{w=4} \\\\  & =(2-(-2))\\cdot (1-0)\\cdot (16-1) \\\\  & =60.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Volume of a Region Using a Change of Variables"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204089 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203852 :question/id #uuid "fd07ad0f-548a-5569-84de-d658a88c4b9c" :question/math-academy-id "e-17087" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$\n\n- $2z - 5 = z + 1$\n\n- $3z - 2 = 3z - 2$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4z+3 & =4z+9 \\\\ 4z+3-4z & =4z+9-4z \\\\ 3 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $4z + 3 = 4z + 9$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-5 & =z+1 \\\\ 2z-5-z+5 & =z+1-z+5 \\\\ z & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $2z - 5 = z + 1$ has the unique solution $z = 6.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-2 & =3z-2 \\\\ 3z-2-3z & =3z-2-3z \\\\ -2 & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d6226fdb-ee73-5a78-87d5-9d6a988a9eb4" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204600 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "957f5ab3-d388-5802-b6c6-5558fc72d19f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203505} {:answer/id #uuid "073992da-065c-598a-8f0c-75308325aff4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203506} {:answer/id #uuid "266a49b8-bc53-50c0-822f-5b3fdc58778c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203507} {:answer/id #uuid "289f48b6-c744-5b4c-ac43-bb7c3b60ead4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203508} {:answer/id #uuid "46217fe4-5804-5c9e-ae53-96c797906451" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203509}] :answer-field/id #uuid "110de02f-a175-5ac8-8341-249e977c4adf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203504}] :question/id #uuid "d5d365bc-e325-5d06-ae8b-3799791feb02" :question/math-academy-id "q-287964" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n1. $2x = x + 1$\n2. $2x + 1 = 2x + 1$\n3. $2x + 1 = 2x + 3$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204601 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85183c94-e141-50ef-945b-894dbe2fe6be" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203511} {:answer/id #uuid "99928875-8585-539b-a856-da07dd6fd1a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203512} {:answer/id #uuid "df332339-0c54-528c-9ada-ee89985519d1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203513} {:answer/id #uuid "6d2d8ffc-a69d-5fdb-b341-6e99f1e6830b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203514} {:answer/id #uuid "75aac1bc-0989-5fdf-a037-6f51b15ba95e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203515}] :answer-field/id #uuid "fa0b4d3b-494b-55d4-9523-196ee64019a4" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203510}] :question/id #uuid "6bda3cab-2f65-5bfd-a054-8baee9c84dc6" :question/math-academy-id "q-288058" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n1. $4x - 2 = 4x + 3$\n2. $3x + 4 = 3x + 4$\n3. $5x = 2x + 6$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297172 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "60c8ed87-cb43-5db0-be5c-e831e6121bd2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295822} {:answer/id #uuid "70fd28f2-8ceb-5fae-b21c-0d8c37373df4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295886} {:answer/id #uuid "992814b5-63ad-57d1-9bda-8a6d0aea5b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296088} {:answer/id #uuid "efc85614-0678-5ede-96c5-eeebb1586b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296543} {:answer/id #uuid "fdc665d1-d05c-5d6a-bc20-c9ca8c023c4e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296616}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "efc85614-0678-5ede-96c5-eeebb1586b84" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296543} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a661465-5e98-54ef-82ac-034599d77271" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296959}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b892079-10a1-5d68-a34f-01695fac566e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288066" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $-3u+4=-3u-1$\n- $2u+5=2u+8$\n- $6u-2=3u+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3u+4 & =-3u-1 \\\\ -3u+4+3u & =-3u-1+3u \\\\ 4 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $-3u+4=-3u-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2u+5 & =2u+8 \\\\ 2u+5-2u & =2u+8-2u \\\\ 5 & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $u.$ Therefore, the equation $2u+5=2u+8$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6u-2 & =3u+1 \\\\ 6u-2-3u+2 & =3u+1-3u+2 \\\\ 3u & =3 \\\\ u & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $6u-2=3u+1$ has the unique solution $u=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297173 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e935809-7eff-5524-8f2e-8f4f21f5d685" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295440} {:answer/id #uuid "276266b0-8b3d-5094-8f78-2c2d0df618f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295484} {:answer/id #uuid "5bc10199-d63c-5736-b2b0-1c0ef77650f1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295796} {:answer/id #uuid "e00d1820-af62-5599-b6e8-6dccf62f726a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296455} {:answer/id #uuid "ff69cb28-f522-54a1-8193-c2a2130eae30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "276266b0-8b3d-5094-8f78-2c2d0df618f5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295484} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f3788ac-8209-560a-917f-1cc30dcfba9c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296927}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9e1fdbd8-4927-548c-aafb-62f0ed007aa2" :question/math-academy-id "q-288070" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $-4y+2=-4y-5$\n- $6y+3=6y-1$\n- $5y-7=2y+1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4y+2 & =-4y-5 \\\\ -4y+2+4y & =-4y-5+4y \\\\ 2 & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $-4y+2=-4y-5$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6y+3 & =6y-1 \\\\ 6y+3-6y & =6y-1-6y \\\\ 3 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $y.$ Therefore, the equation $6y+3=6y-1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5y-7 & =2y+1 \\\\ 5y-7-2y+7 & =2y+1-2y+7 \\\\ 3y & =8 \\\\ y & =\\frac{8}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $5y-7=2y+1$ has the unique solution $y=\\frac{8}{3}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297174 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29f682ce-814d-576c-9d68-46eaffd55e36" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295506} {:answer/id #uuid "34d91129-90ea-5ce1-ba6a-b94c177abdbd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295562} {:answer/id #uuid "5d99b17d-1345-5edc-934a-f0e572fd837c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295806} {:answer/id #uuid "e2b35403-5d7a-5721-b22e-eb61699d25cb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296474} {:answer/id #uuid "f194dee2-3414-5e50-8807-b8317095ddfd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296552}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d99b17d-1345-5edc-934a-f0e572fd837c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295806} :answer-field/id #uuid "03c9470b-a03d-5e8c-a6cc-e7fb12154e33" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296645}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "22403a37-d9b4-5866-bee2-a28d9c27c3b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-288072" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $2z-4=2z+1$\n- $5z+6=5z+6$\n- $3z+2=z+4$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2z-4 & =2z+1 \\\\ 2z-4-2z & =2z+1-2z \\\\ -4 & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $z.$ Therefore, the equation $2z-4=2z+1$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5z+6 & =5z+6 \\\\ 5z+6-5z & =5z+6-5z \\\\ 6 & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $z.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z+2 & =z+4 \\\\ 3z+2-z-2 & =z+4-z-2 \\\\ 2z & =2 \\\\ z & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3z+2=z+4$ has the unique solution $z=1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297175 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "20ec15b0-9006-57c3-a6ed-6a7ffae1f559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295450} {:answer/id #uuid "483443d7-d949-50ec-a7b8-e99c24e9079f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295680} {:answer/id #uuid "8c0712b9-1553-53a1-83a6-c73d8f9df552" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296012} {:answer/id #uuid "8f108ee3-0807-5637-839d-91c354641014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296029} {:answer/id #uuid "9d46e37c-b28f-5c45-9927-cdb6e5e1fe53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296107}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8f108ee3-0807-5637-839d-91c354641014" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296029} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf8696e8-0827-512c-9081-1eb13fb3de19" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297063}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ae9bca83-e68f-5756-9519-1447b4343c01" :question/math-academy-id "q-288073" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $3w+2=2w+2$\n- $4w-7=4w-7$\n- $6w+5=6w+9$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3w+2 & =2w+2 \\\\ 3w+2-2w-2 & =2w+2-2w-2 \\\\ w & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $3w+2=2w+2$ has the unique solution $w=0.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4w-7 & =4w-7 \\\\ 4w-7-4w & =4w-7-4w \\\\ -7 & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $w.$\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6w+5 & =6w+9 \\\\ 6w+5-6w & =6w+9-6w \\\\ 5 & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $w.$ Therefore, the equation $6w+5=6w+9$ has no solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297176 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d604b35-b182-51ef-8ad8-153a353f0418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295529} {:answer/id #uuid "3d2e8c0b-8871-52a8-8f7e-27c536e84a13" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295618} {:answer/id #uuid "560da999-55c4-5b56-a1d6-481d32f3a37f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295765} {:answer/id #uuid "a943da63-fb2b-5fdc-9bc3-6074acd881a9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296157} {:answer/id #uuid "f7c83eab-72fe-5dac-9261-1a12e8b33cf2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296585}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d604b35-b182-51ef-8ad8-153a353f0418" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295529} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a845396-80e0-59b7-bded-5ac38a7616cc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296777}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6480ee2c-c900-5448-85b3-32f94538971e" :question/math-academy-id "q-288074" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions?\n\n- $9v-5=4v+2$\n- $2v+3=2v+7$\n- $5v-1=5v-1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's attempt to solve each equation in turn:\n\n- For the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9v-5 & =4v+2 \\\\ 9v-5-4v+5 & =4v+2-4v+5 \\\\ 5v & =7 \\\\ v & =\\frac{7}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation $9v-5=4v+2$ has the unique solution $v=\\frac{7}{5}.$\n\n- For the second equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v+3 & =2v+7 \\\\ 2v+3-2v & =2v+7-2v \\\\ 3 & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is false for all values of $v.$ Therefore, the equation $2v+3=2v+7$ has no solutions.\n\n- For the third equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5v-1 & =5v-1 \\\\ 5v-1-5v & =5v-1-5v \\\\ -1 & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis equation is true for all values of $v.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\""}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Equation With No Solutions"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204090 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203853 :question/id #uuid "cba9196c-fdc3-594d-a1ad-948cf75bd366" :question/math-academy-id "e-17213" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4(r - 2)y = -8\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay = b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na = 4(r - 2), \\qquad b = -8.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4(r - 2) = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(r-2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{4(r-2)}{4} & =\\frac{0}{4} \\\\ r-2 & =0 \\\\ r & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c0e7426e-553d-5f20-811f-15f3a5ea0056" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204596 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8639ce62-5e23-5361-b682-c7000582d54e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203491} {:answer/id #uuid "f6b1d5cd-7280-5a3b-b538-d976044c6e94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203492} {:answer/id #uuid "fcfa0123-90e9-5186-b54d-3ae7859972b8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203493} {:answer/id #uuid "3cb6287c-2e23-513c-b813-c514eaaaaae3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203494} {:answer/id #uuid "7dffe079-6e58-5eee-a2d4-f50f9c6c606c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203495}] :answer-field/id #uuid "b9422b9e-c65d-596a-90ac-20fde363b97c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203490}] :question/id #uuid "b6c453dd-3b6b-5f65-bf37-1fc261d1f26a" :question/math-academy-id "q-287665" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n$2px = 3$\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1ab2ed61-9a09-5b11-8a3a-164d58ee2841" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295425} {:answer/id #uuid "3661811c-5e94-5d73-b96b-461bf4e8e913" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295572} {:answer/id #uuid "40b4c162-fdcc-5a2f-b9e5-cbff3a9f1ba0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295638} {:answer/id #uuid "47a2b01c-7509-53de-83a6-61b8b988976f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295675} {:answer/id #uuid "9875c009-5dc6-5f7c-aa43-8e5947cacf7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296084}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "47a2b01c-7509-53de-83a6-61b8b988976f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295675} :answer-field/id #uuid "66a0300a-10ae-558b-8e38-4e29591d58b3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296846}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "328a41df-8074-5d3e-9600-d58da9a2fa99" :question/math-academy-id "q-288593" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q-1)y=4\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=q-1,\\,b=4.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq-1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q-1 & =0 \\\\ q & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297177 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a358628-bbab-5730-aa92-30852d8b8b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295319} {:answer/id #uuid "13781b68-e9a2-5ebc-b3a8-d9a9f51cced6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295375} {:answer/id #uuid "28fc5cf6-6471-5d82-bb36-92f61e00662f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295498} {:answer/id #uuid "38232641-7f92-55ce-8842-067f6f7fcb51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295582} {:answer/id #uuid "a107bd3a-590a-5785-8899-90e2f9d4863d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0a358628-bbab-5730-aa92-30852d8b8b73" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295319} :answer-field/id #uuid "b26af55d-71ac-5eea-97e8-97cf35cab8c3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297009}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e5cf9073-af1e-5711-af30-8567dad9e870" :question/math-academy-id "q-288135" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n4py=-2\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=4p,\\,b=-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n4p=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\np=0.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297178 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a9cd574-87f6-5684-a4a2-52f23dcc4f6d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295323} {:answer/id #uuid "4512c30d-0418-5d1a-a9a4-5767ffaddbf8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295659} {:answer/id #uuid "4e445b8e-50d5-51d4-9056-86f12aba889d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295718} {:answer/id #uuid "967246bb-eb10-50c8-9141-55173f83e32f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296068} {:answer/id #uuid "b2ae615f-ae70-595f-a21b-63e9d4cc1379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296210}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b2ae615f-ae70-595f-a21b-63e9d4cc1379" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296210} :answer-field/id #uuid "078b8df7-1fda-503a-8213-ba030a04d1b7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296656}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9d84e977-5f76-5e6a-86a0-a01877993e64" :question/math-academy-id "q-288143" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)x=6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ax=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-3(q+4),\\,b=6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3(q+4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3(q+4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-3(q+4)}{-3} & =\\frac{0}{-3} \\\\ q+4 & =0 \\\\ q & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297179 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f0eba8c-0b29-5b5a-b4c3-fc5fa03d4285" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295356} {:answer/id #uuid "13856c1d-6d03-503c-9205-0f5ed6cb4b42" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295377} {:answer/id #uuid "1f48924c-20c5-5007-956d-ee2c534ab597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295444} {:answer/id #uuid "282c0cbc-ece4-5cc6-a26a-a2c131358e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295489} {:answer/id #uuid "e0ca9a90-0103-50a4-9b62-d04c30db7ae1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296464}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "282c0cbc-ece4-5cc6-a26a-a2c131358e93" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295489} :answer-field/id #uuid "137f01ce-55b5-52a3-a12a-51efb4599a5a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296675}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1bd38fc7-d1ae-53ac-8f14-e2748bd742a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-288144" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-5(p-9)z=10\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $az=b.$ In this case, \n\n$$\na=-5(p-9),\\,b=10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-5(p-9)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5(p-9) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-5(p-9)}{-5} & =\\frac{0}{-5} \\\\ p-9 & =0 \\\\ p & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297197 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "298bad1f-9367-5a05-95d2-933a09daee2e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{9}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295501} {:answer/id #uuid "3dad73b7-975d-5246-83b7-9657d612712e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295623} {:answer/id #uuid "a52921f6-35e3-5fd6-831b-5c76a68fe597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} {:answer/id #uuid "c10c0188-b833-5221-be57-af73a4dae17b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296286} {:answer/id #uuid "f34978c9-f088-5aab-b438-960ba47ee1ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296559}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a52921f6-35e3-5fd6-831b-5c76a68fe597" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296138} :answer-field/id #uuid "aab0add4-30b0-59ee-8400-8a7cc1beaf29" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296994}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1328ee8c-fdcc-59c5-a877-6291af207d84" :question/math-academy-id "q-288596" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q+9)y=-6\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nNotice that this equation is in the form $ay=b.$ In this case,\n\n$$\na=q+9,\\,b=-6.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq+9=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q+9 & =0 \\\\ q & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204091 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203878 :question/id #uuid "5ee14499-89b0-59a3-9f13-b0a00c0e518d" :question/math-academy-id "e-18853" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n(r - 9)z + 2 = -1\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $az = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-9)z+2 & =-1 \\\\ (r-9)z+2-2 & =-1-2 \\\\ {{(r-9)}_{⏟}}_{a}z & ={{-3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nr - 9 = 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r-9 & =0 \\\\ r & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ba644b27-7245-5b7b-8cd6-2493836a7d03" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204597 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2937687-dedd-5964-bb4d-22153fc13d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296297}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2937687-dedd-5964-bb4d-22153fc13d86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296297} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a06a891-d4f2-506a-bdd0-0692d7f6be31" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296775}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "38670cb7-04d7-5bd3-bbc0-32b596369870" :question/math-academy-id "q-287667" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2(p-4)x+2=5\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4)x+2 & =5 \\\\ 2(p-4)x+2-2 & =5-2 \\\\ {{2(p-4)}_{⏟}}_{a}x & ={{3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2(p-4)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(p-4) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{2(p-4)}{2} & =\\frac{0}{2} \\\\ p-4 & =0 \\\\ p & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204603 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "88983c17-8c0d-5f28-8a83-c178d5d425cd" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203522}] :question/id #uuid "eafa17e2-08f9-58c7-8e3f-be7946a2edcf" :question/math-academy-id "q-288643" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z$, given by\n$(r + 3)z + 5 =-4$\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r$?$r =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297201 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9611e30a-239c-5cbf-b771-57108c2e379b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9611e30a-239c-5cbf-b771-57108c2e379b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296065} :answer-field/id #uuid "28c6d002-88fb-5a97-a562-15802c967ffc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296712}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d326f3de-7c8d-596a-800c-00170de0cabb" :question/math-academy-id "q-288634" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n3(p+2)x+7=10\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2)x+7 & =10 \\\\ 3(p+2)x+7-7 & =10-7 \\\\ {{3(p+2)}_{⏟}}_{a}x & ={{3}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3(p+2)=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3(p+2) & =0 \\\\ \\frac{3(p+2)}{3} & =\\frac{0}{3} \\\\ p+2 & =0 \\\\ p & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297202 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "488785de-3552-5c44-b37c-f80db84151a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295682}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "488785de-3552-5c44-b37c-f80db84151a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295682} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f518425-6bb3-513e-9a2f-cd6d4cd2d157" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296784}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e9e21b0c-1002-52c2-8ff0-0958a8c28640" :question/math-academy-id "q-288644" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $v,$ given by\n\n$$\n(k-2)v-3=5\n$$\n\nwhere $k$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $k?$\n\n$k=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $av=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k-2)v-3 & =5 \\\\ (k-2)v-3+3 & =5+3 \\\\ {{(k-2)}_{⏟}}_{a}v & ={{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nk-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $k,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}k-2 & =0 \\\\ k & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297203 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1b250822-5029-5ba9-b12a-849c9b646e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295427}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1b250822-5029-5ba9-b12a-849c9b646e53" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295427} :answer-field/id #uuid "48bda282-6138-518c-9c17-6cf01e4a366f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296768}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "219bc978-7d21-5b4f-a212-8daca1973e88" :question/math-academy-id "q-288646" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n(m+6)x+2=7\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(m+6)x+2 & =7 \\\\ (m+6)x+2-2 & =7-2 \\\\ {{(m+6)}_{⏟}}_{a}x & ={{5}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nm+6=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m+6 & =0 \\\\ m & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297204 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "44288224-92ba-5f1b-8f40-4d5d14a53492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295653}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "44288224-92ba-5f1b-8f40-4d5d14a53492" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295653} :answer-field/id #uuid "07852a03-fe73-5e40-83b8-89647ce1bddb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296655}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f6400a6-9f87-55b0-bcbe-d4a53fe5731a" :question/math-academy-id "q-288648" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $u,$ given by\n\n$$\n(q-5)u+9=0\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, let's write our equation in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(q-5)u+9 & =0 \\\\ (q-5)u+9-9 & =0-9 \\\\ {{(q-5)}_{⏟}}_{a}u & ={{-9}_{⏟}}_{b}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\nq-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}q-5 & =0 \\\\ q & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Constants on Both Sides"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204092 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186203854 :question/id #uuid "36eadc0b-c2b0-5f84-8f39-2b8399932edb" :question/math-academy-id "e-17214" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x,$ given by\n\n$$\n2nx + 7  = -4x + 5\n$$\n\nwhere $n$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $n?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax = b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\neq 0.$ If $a = 0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ax = b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $4x$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+7 & =-4x+5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =-4x+5+4x \\\\ 2nx+4x+7 & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $7$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2nx+4x+7 & =5 \\\\ 2nx+4x+7-7 & =5-7 \\\\ 2nx+4x & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $x$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(2n + 4)x  = -2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax = b{:}$\n\n$$\n\\underbrace{(2n + 4)}_{a}x  = \\underbrace{-2}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n2n + 4= 0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $n,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2n+4 & =0 \\\\ 2n+4-4 & =0-4 \\\\ 2n & =-4 \\\\ \\frac{2n}{2} & =\\frac{-4}{2} \\\\ n & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d63847ef-6360-50b5-b585-9084140b2d67" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a0f08b77-eeef-5fc2-8543-09a2276b46e9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203498} {:answer/id #uuid "ed88b415-ec31-5753-9538-94d29a0edac6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203499} {:answer/id #uuid "06e406e9-458f-5b5b-8a76-47ae321cb09d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203500} {:answer/id #uuid "217b4c73-d235-5933-8a9f-ef748ad498ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203501} {:answer/id #uuid "7cc66499-02a0-5560-9357-f491dc4ae661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203502}] :answer-field/id #uuid "204618ec-8108-58ed-8408-6f51fe3d50c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203497}] :question/id #uuid "c5eb239a-6da7-5d84-946d-c53824d57aa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-287677" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n$2px = 3x + 5$\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p$?"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186204599 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/id #uuid "41d7cf18-e5fd-5caa-873f-517786f6a348" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186203503}] :question/id #uuid "13915c55-7c7c-53b4-adee-86cf0ce79ae1" :question/math-academy-id "q-287678" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $x$, given by\n$2px + 6 = 4x + 5$\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p$?$p =$\n{{field-1}}"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297205 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ae663b6-85c5-5077-b87c-a6180d34333b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295510} {:answer/id #uuid "4b4fa932-686f-5272-80f1-319a4616fc55" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295695} {:answer/id #uuid "5513a93f-ae93-5de6-bdce-765af89d5be4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295759} {:answer/id #uuid "cdfab9aa-d797-5176-8659-fc606759afe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296346} {:answer/id #uuid "f950fb0f-6b6e-53ca-bbe7-61f27fce1157" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296591}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdfab9aa-d797-5176-8659-fc606759afe0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296346} :answer-field/id #uuid "1839f0af-c2f8-56ec-a3e2-a0f1a0ec6286" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296681}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6cf4ece6-fb8b-5a6f-963d-f97832f4331b" :question/math-academy-id "q-288662" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $y,$ given by\n\n$$\n3py=5y+8\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $ay=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $5y$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3py & =5y+8 \\\\ 3py-5y & =5y+8-5y \\\\ 3py-5y & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $y$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3p-5)y=8.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ay=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(3p-5)}_{⏟}}_{a}y={{8}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3p-5=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3p-5 & =0 \\\\ 3p-5+5 & =0+5 \\\\ 3p & =5 \\\\ \\frac{3p}{3} & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{5}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297206 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9acb3749-840c-51ad-9eb4-ee8c920e6230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9acb3749-840c-51ad-9eb4-ee8c920e6230" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296092} :answer-field/id #uuid "31011122-f1db-5927-9c5b-95335e1dd8b2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fa4e94a9-eff0-5fbb-b2d3-1dcbd9a3e5ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-288673" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n3qz+4=7z+1\n$$\n\nwhere $q$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $q?$\n\n$q=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $7z$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz+4 & =7z+1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =7z+1-7z \\\\ 3qz-7z+4 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $4$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3qz-7z+4 & =1 \\\\ 3qz-7z+4-4 & =1-4 \\\\ 3qz-7z & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(3q-7)z=-3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $az=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(3q-7)}_{⏟}}_{a}z={{-3}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n3q-7=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $q,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3q-7 & =0 \\\\ 3q-7+7 & =0+7 \\\\ 3q & =7 \\\\ \\frac{3q}{3} & =\\frac{7}{3} \\\\ q & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297207 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03a617fe-2ec0-59b8-aa7e-a8f0c47af481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295282}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "03a617fe-2ec0-59b8-aa7e-a8f0c47af481" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295282} :answer-field/id #uuid "52022133-680b-5a4f-b274-743fc5a96fa9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296797}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9c5e8ea4-ee94-5a1f-a3c1-cf0a3b495462" :question/math-academy-id "q-288674" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $u,$ given by\n\n$$\n5ru+3=2u+6\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $r?$\n\n$r=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $au=b.$ \n\nWe start by subtracting $2u$ from both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ru+3 & =2u+6 \\\\ 5ru-2u+3 & =2u+6-2u \\\\ 5ru-2u+3 & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $3$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5ru-2u+3 & =6 \\\\ 5ru-2u+3-3 & =6-3 \\\\ 5ru-2u & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $u$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(5r-2)u=3.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $au=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(5r-2)}_{⏟}}_{a}u={{3}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n5r-2=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $r,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5r-2 & =0 \\\\ 5r-2+2 & =0+2 \\\\ 5r & =2 \\\\ \\frac{5r}{5} & =\\frac{2}{5} \\\\ r & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297208 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d96f33ba-5b23-51ab-944b-35f7ed28804d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296414}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d96f33ba-5b23-51ab-944b-35f7ed28804d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296414} :answer-field/id #uuid "fb1883d7-5f64-5b1c-9d6c-398589b85515" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186297148}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "169f6fd4-529f-5b4e-9fcd-e754c540805a" :question/math-academy-id "q-288675" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $w,$ given by\n\n$$\n-4mw+5=-3w+1\n$$\n\nwhere $m$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $m?$\n\n$m=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $aw=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $3w$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4mw+5 & =-3w+1 \\\\ -4mw+3w+5 & =-3w+1+3w \\\\ -4mw+3w+5 & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we subtract $5$ from both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4mw+3w+5 & =1 \\\\ -4mw+3w+5-5 & =1-5 \\\\ -4mw+3w & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $w$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-4m+3)w=-4.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $aw=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(-4m+3)}_{⏟}}_{a}w={{-4}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-4m+3=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $m,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-4m+3 & =0 \\\\ -4m+3-3 & =0-3 \\\\ -4m & =-3 \\\\ \\frac{-4m}{-4} & =\\frac{-3}{-4} \\\\ m & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186297209 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38f1f5d0-a65b-50e5-ae53-71603b6a4f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295592}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38f1f5d0-a65b-50e5-ae53-71603b6a4f59" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186295592} :answer-field/id #uuid "0392835c-e04b-5ad0-8c8d-d5dc8f5281c2" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186296644}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "93ece185-5396-5f04-8ab8-086b4b609861" :question/math-academy-id "q-288678" :question/problem "Consider the equation in the variable $z,$ given by\n\n$$\n-3pz-8=-z-6\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is a constant. Given that this equation has no solution, what is the value of $p?$\n\n$p=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Suppose we have an equation of the form\n\n$$\nax=b\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants, and $b\\ne 0.$ If $a=0,$ then the equation has no solution.\n\nFirst, we rewrite the equation in the form $az=b.$ \n\nWe start by adding $z$ to both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz-8 & =-z-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-z-6+z \\\\ -3pz+z-8 & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we add $8$ to both sides:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3pz+z-8 & =-6 \\\\ -3pz+z-8+8 & =-6+8 \\\\ -3pz+z & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we factor $z$ on the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n(-3p+1)z=2.\n$$\n\nNotice that this equation is now in the form $ax=b:$\n\n$$\n{{(-3p+1)}_{⏟}}_{a}z={{2}_{⏟}}_{b}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equation has no solution if\n\n$$\n-3p+1=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3p+1 & =0 \\\\ -3p+1-1 & =0-1 \\\\ -3p & =-1 \\\\ \\frac{-3p}{-3} & =\\frac{-1}{-3} \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Solving For an Unknown Parameter - Variables and Constants on Both Sides"}]}
